    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p6-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Write down the first 10 perfect squares.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1,4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81,100$.",
          "worked": "The first ten positive integers squared give\n$$1^2,2^2,3^2,4^2,5^2,6^2,7^2,8^2,9^2,10^2,$$\nso the first ten perfect squares are\n$$1,4,9,16,25,36,49,64,81,100.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "From this list, find an arithmetic sequence of three perfect squares.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1,25,49$, with common difference $24$.",
          "worked": "$1,25,49$ are perfect squares from the list.\n$$\\begin{align*}25-1&=24\\\\49-25&=24\\end{align*}$$\nThe consecutive differences are equal, so this is an arithmetic sequence.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q12-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Let $(a,b,c)$ be positive integers for which $a^2+b^2=c^2$. Prove that $(b-a)^2,c^2,(b+a)^2$ is an arithmetic sequence of perfect squares.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both consecutive differences are $2ab$.\n\nUse $a^2+b^2=c^2$ to simplify each difference.",
          "worked": "Since $a,b,c$ are integers, all three terms are perfect squares. Using $a^2+b^2=c^2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}c^2-(b-a)^2&=c^2-(b^2-2ab+a^2)\\\\c^2-(b-a)^2&=(c^2-a^2-b^2)+2ab\\\\c^2-(b-a)^2&=2ab.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\begin{align*}(b+a)^2-c^2&=b^2+2ab+a^2-c^2\\\\(b+a)^2-c^2&=(a^2+b^2-c^2)+2ab\\\\(b+a)^2-c^2&=2ab.\\end{align*}$$\nThe consecutive differences are equal, so the terms form an arithmetic sequence of perfect squares.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q12-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Hence, find an arithmetic sequence of perfect squares whose middle term is 169.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$49,169,289$.\n\nUse the Pythagorean triple $(5,12,13)$ in part (c).",
          "worked": "The middle term is $169=13^2$. Since $5^2+12^2=13^2$, part (c) gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(12-5)^2&=49\\\\13^2&=169\\\\(12+5)^2&=289.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $49,169,289$ is an arithmetic sequence of perfect squares, with common difference $120$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
